> Derive the matrix iteration and its stopping test

Let A_uv count supplied copies of the edge u to v. Loops and repeated edges are
accepted; duplicates act as integer weights. With N(x)=x/||x||_2 for a nonzero
vector and N(0)=0, iteration k is

$$a_k=N(A^T h_{k-1}),\qquad h_k=N(Aa_k).$$
\alttext(First normalize the transpose adjacency product with previous hubs, then normalize the adjacency product with current authorities.)

Both initial vectors contain 1/sqrt(n), or are empty for n=0. Normalizing the
authority product only scales the subsequent hub product, so whenever products
are nonzero the hub direction follows power iteration on AA^T. Authorities
follow A^T h with a half-step offset; A^T A describes their corresponding power
operator.

For a symmetric positive-semidefinite operator, a positive gap between its
largest eigenvalue and the remaining spectrum gives geometric directional
convergence from an initialization having a nonzero dominant projection.
Repeated largest eigenvalues allow several dominant directions. The supplied
uniform initialization determines a component mixture; small update change alone
does not establish uniqueness or relevance of a ranking.

The actual termination test is

$$\delta_k=\max_i\{ |a_{k,i}-a_{k-1,i}|, |h_{k,i}-h_{k-1,i}|\}<\varepsilon.$$
\alttext(The largest absolute component change across both vectors must be strictly below the positive tolerance.)

The finite positive tolerance is checked before policy caps and allocation. The
update budget can be zero: that returns initialization without computing graph
scores or applying a stopping test. Each normalization accumulates squared
doubles sequentially in vertex order, takes a square root, and divides only for
a positive norm. This retains the historical arithmetic order; it is not a
scaled norm algorithm or a certified eigenvector solver. Nonfinite tolerance or
norm arithmetic is rejected.

> The original graph and its closed-form reference

Keep the original edges 0 to 2, 0 to 3, and 1 to 2. Restricting rows to hubs 0,1
and columns to authorities 2,3 gives B=[[1,1],[1,0]]. Therefore
BB^T=[[2,1],[1,1]], with eigenvalues (3+sqrt(5))/2 and (3-sqrt(5))/2. Its
dominant normalized vector is (phi,1)/sqrt(phi^2+1), where phi=(1+sqrt(5))/2.
Both nonzero hub and authority pairs approach (0.850650808,0.525731112).

The first raw authority pair is (1,1/2); the first normalized hub pair is
(3,2)/sqrt(13). In this fixture the subdominant/dominant eigenvalue ratio is
about 0.145898, explaining its rapid directional contraction. The plot compares
actual recorded values with the derived limit; it does not replace update
history with that formula.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hits_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::hits_trace(4, {{0, 2}, {0, 3}, {1, 2}});
    const double phi =
        (1 + std::sqrt(5.)) / 2, ref = phi / std::sqrt(phi * phi + 1);
    std::print("| vertex | actual hubs | actual authorities |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n", i, t.run.result.hubs[i],
                   t.run.result.authorities[i]);
    std::print("\nPaired updates {}; last change {:.4e}; tolerance branch {}.\n",
               t.run.result.iterations, t.run.last_change,
               t.run.stop == dm::algorithms::HitsStop::Tolerance);
    std::vector<double> x, h, a, limit;
    std::print("\n| update | joint change | hub-0 absolute reference error | authority-2 absolute reference error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &step : t.steps) {
        x.push_back(step.iteration);
        h.push_back(step.hubs[0]);
        a.push_back(step.authorities[2]);
        limit.push_back(ref);
        std::print("| {} | {:.4e} | {:.4e} | {:.4e} |\n", step.iteration,
                   step.change, std::abs(step.hubs[0] - ref),
                   std::abs(step.authorities[2] - ref));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, h, "hub 0", {37, 99, 235}, 2.).line(x, a, "authority 2",
                                                  {220, 38, 38}, 2.).line(
        x, limit, "analytic limit", {22, 163, 74}, 1.5).title(
        "Original graph: actual alternating scores").x_label(
        "complete paired updates").y_label("normalized score").y_limits(.82, .94).save_svg("build/hits_original.svg");
    if (std::abs(t.run.result.hubs[0] - ref) > 1e-12 ||
        std::abs(t.run.result.authorities[2] - ref) > 1e-12)
        throw std::runtime_error("closed form");
}
