// result_end
\caption(Zero vectors, uniform initialization, weighted endorsements and tied components have distinct meanings despite sharing one API.)
\alttext(Hub scores for the five control cases show zeros, initial one-half values, original and duplicate-weighted scores, and equal tied-component scores.)

> Validation and reference

The native tests independently reconstruct dense transpose/forward products for
all 512 directed binary three-vertex graphs, including loops, and check every
captured raw vector, normalization, change and work count. Separate controls
check exact and one-short admission, invalid tolerance/endpoints, deep
ownership, empty and zero-budget behavior, duplicate edges and the analytic
four-vertex solution. ASan/UBSan exercise the producing kernel. Frozen
pre-conversion comparisons separately check legacy outputs and iteration counts.
Compiled documentation examples, symbol hovers, actual MEP runs, inspected
native SVGs and byte-checked Markdown/HTML bundles form the report conversion
gates.

The mathematical motivation is [Jon M. Kleinberg, Authoritative Sources in a
Hyperlinked Environment](https://www.cs.cornell.edu/home/kleinber/auth.pdf),
published in JACM 46(5), 1999, [DOI
10.1145/324133.324140](https://doi.org/10.1145/324133.324140). The paper builds
query-focused subgraphs and discusses link heuristics. This report executes only
the supplied fixed graph, with the explicit duplicate/self-edge conventions
above; it performs no web crawl or query-dependent base-set construction.
